From 216e421c373b85804c4fc910b7bac6ee51b2fba7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arya Alikhani <alexanderaryaalikhani@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 10:21:56 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 17/17] boringssl: size ECH GREASE payloads from the
 ClientHello

Firefox's GREASE encrypted_client_hello payload depends on the
ClientHello that carries it: every retained Firefox 156 and 157
capture has 240 bytes on a fresh connection and 368 bytes on a resumed
one, over TCP and QUIC. Patch 0011 can only fix one length per
connection.

NSS 3.128 sizes the payload in tls13_MaybeGreaseEch, after the other
extensions, including pre_shared_key with placeholder binders, are
built and before padding. It builds the EncodedClientHelloInner that a
real ECH offer would encrypt: server_name and pre_shared_key in full,
supported_versions with TLS 1.3 and any GREASE version, no extension
that NSS supports but does not know in TLS 1.3 (ec_point_formats,
extended_master_secret, session_ticket, renegotiation_info), and every
other extension compressed into ech_outer_extensions. tls13_PadChInner
then adds the maximum name length minus the length of the URL host
(strlen(ss->url)), rounds to a multiple of 32, and NSS adds a 16-byte
tag. The maximum name length is NSS's default of 100; Firefox applies
security.tls.ech.grease_size, also 100, over TCP only.

NSS sends no server_name for an IP literal but still pads by its text.
Firefox 157, pointed at https://127.0.0.1:<port>/ and
https://[::1]:<port>/, sends GREASE ECH without server_name (240 bytes
over TCP, 208 over QUIC for IPv4). Over TCP, setting grease_size so
that one byte of host length crosses a 32-byte boundary showed it pads
by 9 for 127.0.0.1 and 3 for ::1, the host without brackets. QUIC
ignores grease_size, and its fresh payload cannot tell those lengths
apart.

Add SSL_set_ech_grease_payload_from_client_hello, which takes the
maximum name length and the host text NSS pads by; without a host it
uses the server_name host name, or no name. setup_ech_grease runs once
per handshake, after PSKs, key shares, the extension order, and the
early-data decision are known, so it writes the unencrypted ClientHello
without its ECH extension, padding, and binders, and applies the rule;
a HelloRetryRequest reuses the extension. The last call between this
function and SSL_set_ech_grease_payload_length wins. DTLS is rejected,
as NSS does not GREASE ECH there.

ssl_test checks fixed vectors shaped like each Firefox 157 ClientHello
(240 and 368 bytes over TCP and QUIC, and the IP-literal captures at
the boundary grease sizes), and compares fresh, resumed, IP-literal,
HelloRetryRequest, and QUIC ClientHellos against an independent model
of the NSS rule, because the resumed length depends on the ticket.
Outside the patch, the rule reproduced the payload length of all 171
retained Firefox 156.0, 156.0.1, and 157.0 ClientHellos.
